[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Sakura is installed but will not start until you point a virtual terminal at
it. Both steps below have a ready-made snippet in %%PREFIX%%/etc/sakura.

1. Append the "sakura" entry to /etc/gettytab:

     cat %%PREFIX%%/etc/sakura/gettytab.example >> /etc/gettytab

2. Replace the ttyv1 line in /etc/ttys with the one in ttys.example, so
   init(8) starts getty(8) with that entry:

     ttyv1  "/usr/libexec/getty sakura"  xterm  onifexists  secure

3. Apply the change:

     kill -HUP 1

Sakura then owns virtual terminal 2 (/dev/ttyv1), reachable with Alt-F2.

Before rebooting, keep a second virtual terminal with a normal getty on it and
confirm you can still log in there. A mistake in /etc/ttys can otherwise leave
the machine with no way to log in, recoverable only from single-user mode.

Configuration is %%PREFIX%%/etc/sakura/config.ini, which documents every
option in place; config.ini.example is a pristine copy. Check a configuration
before relying on it:

     sakura --validate-config %%PREFIX%%/etc/sakura

The wallpaper needs no font work: every console font FreeBSD ships already has
the block glyphs it draws with.
EOM
}
{ type: remove
  message: <<EOM
Sakura is removed, but /etc/ttys and /etc/gettytab still reference it.

Restore the ttyv1 line in /etc/ttys to its stock form:

     ttyv1  "/usr/libexec/getty Pc"  xterm  onifexists  secure

then run "kill -HUP 1". The "sakura" block in /etc/gettytab is inert once
nothing references it and can be left or removed.

Doing this BEFORE rebooting matters: init(8) will otherwise keep trying to
start a program that is no longer installed on that terminal.
EOM
}
]
